Both document.write() and console.log() are JavaScript methods used for displaying output.
But they serve different purposes and display their output in different locations within a web development environment. 

document.write()
----------------
Purpose:- 
Primarily used to display text or HTML content directly onto the web page itself.
Then modifying the page's structure and what users see.
Output Location:- 
The output is visible on the web page within the browser window.
Interaction with the DOM:- 
It directly manipulates the Document Object Model (DOM), altering the structure and content of the webpage.

console.log()
-------------
Purpose:- 
Primarily used by developers for debugging, testing code, and inspecting data in the browser's developer console.
Output Location:- 
The output is displayed in the browser's developer console and is generally not visible to end-users.
Only visible in the browser's developer tools (F12 → Console).
Interaction with the DOM:- 
It does not directly read, write, modify, or update the DOM.
Advantages:-
It provides a valuable tool for tracking the flow of code, checking variable values, and understanding how the program behaves without affecting the user's experience on the web page.
Does not modify the webpage.
It supports multiple various log levels (console.warn(), console.error(), console.info()) for more meaningful debugging messages.
When to use:-
For debugging and testing JavaScript code.
To inspect variable values, function outputs, and data structures at runtime.
To track function calls and their arguments.
To monitor the performance of code blocks.
To inspect DOM elements and their properties in web development. 
